import Native

{-
   The array and its window - `lib/Core/Array.native.yana`, on the terms `Core.yana` states: the
   same heads, the same fields, a subset of the operations.

   **Two records for one type**, and the relationship between them is the compiler's one decision
   about either. `Array(a)` claims `@builtin(array)` and is what `[a]` names: an owner, whose fields
   are this file's. `ArrayView(a)` claims `@builtin(arrayView)` and is what a *window* is - the two
   words a shared or unique `[a]` in a binding position occupies. Nothing resolves `[a]` to it; a
   window is `Array(a)` under a view contract, and the view table in `compiler/resolve_mir/type.cpp`
   is what says the bytes under that contract are this record's. `doc/proposal/Analysis-Manifestations.md`
   Part 8 is the argument.

   `@local` is what makes the view a representation rather than a type: outside this module the name
   cannot be written, and `checkDeclaredRoles` refuses the role on anything that is not local, has
   a second constructor, or owns a field. Inside, it is an ordinary record, and the bodies below that
   build one are ordinary bodies whose result position is a window.
-}
@builtin(array)
pub data Array(a) {run: Run(a), length: Count}

@local @builtin(arrayView)
data ArrayView(a) {items: %a, length: Size}

{-
   What stops the program when a subscript is out of range - `lib/Core/Check.yana`'s `checkFailed`,
   cut down to the one thing it does natively: exit with the status an abort answers with. The
   subscript is `Index`'s member below, so the check is the library's and the compiler inserts none.

   `@builtin(checkFailed)` is not the compiler asking to call this: it never does. It is what lets
   `-no-checks` recognize one of these branches as a check, which is a question about a *callee* and
   is answered against this declaration rather than against the word "checkFailed" - see
   `compiler/resolve_ssa/opt/check.cpp`.
-}
@builtin(checkFailed)
pub fn checkFailed():
    syscall1(231 :: USize, 134 :: USize)
    return

{-
   The extent, the element and the sub-window - the three operations the compiler reads no field of
   a view for. Each is an ordinary body over the view's own fields, and `xs[i]`, `xs[a..b]` and
   `length(xs)` reach them by the class or the role rather than by anything the compiler knows
   about `items` or `length`.

   The bounds test is one unsigned comparison: an index read as `USize` puts a negative one above
   every length there is, which is the same trick every bounds check in every systems language uses.

   **`@inline(always)` on each**, and it is a directive rather than a hint: the body is grafted
   into the caller by the SSA conversion, with no optimizer involved, so `xs[i]` and `length(xs)`
   cost what the two lines say they cost in a `-no-ir-opt` build too. That is what lets these be
   bodies where the compiler used to hold a node - `doc/spec/collections.md`'s promise, kept by the
   library.
-}
instance Length(Array(a)):
    @inline(always) fn length(self: [a]) -> Size = self.length

instance (n: Int) Length([a *n]):
    @inline(always) fn length(self: [a *n]) -> Size = n :: Size

{-
   The element address a window is defined by - `lib/Native/Run.native.yana`'s `values`, and the one
   place a pointer into a window is given the window's provenance.

   A pointer read out of a field is a number with no origin, so a borrow taken through it would be
   rooted in nothing and refused at the result. This signature is what roots it: the result names
   `source`, the same origin the window was borrowed from, and every element borrow below is a
   borrow of a pointer this handed out.
-}
@inline(always)
pub fn values(self: source'[a]) -> %source'a = self.items

{-
   The same address for a caller that may **write** through it - `values`' unique half.

   Two functions rather than one, because the capability is on the *window* and not on the pointer:
   `%source'a` says which storage it points into and nothing about what may be done there, so what
   decides whether a store through it is legal is the loan the window was read under. A shared one
   is what every element accessor takes; this is what a literal fills its slots through, and what
   `known.def`'s `elementBase` role names.
-}
@builtin(elementBase) @inline(always)
pub fn valuesMut(&self: source'[a]) -> %source'a = self.items

instance Index(Array(a), Size, a):
    @inline(always) fn get(self: source'[a], index: Size) -> source'a:
        if (bitcast(index) :: USize) >= (bitcast(self.length) :: USize) then checkFailed()
        return borrow(advance(values(self), index))

    @inline(always) fn getMut(&self: source'[a], index: Size) -> &source'a:
        if (bitcast(index) :: USize) >= (bitcast(self.length) :: USize) then checkFailed()
        return borrowMut(advance(values(self), index))

{-
   `xs[from..to]`, clamped rather than checked: an out-of-range bound produces a shorter window
   instead of a trap, as `lib/Core/Array.yana` argues. `sliceMut` is the same window at the
   capability the caller already had, and `&self` is what makes it sound - the caller's window is
   exclusively borrowed for as long as this one lives.
-}
@builtin(slice) @inline(always)
pub fn slice(self: source'[a], from: Size, to: Size) -> source'[a]:
    let &start = from
    if start < 0 then start = 0
    if start > self.length then start = self.length

    let &end = to
    if end < start then end = start
    if end > self.length then end = self.length

    return ArrayView {items: advance(self.items, start), length: end - start}

@builtin(sliceMut) @inline(always)
pub fn sliceMut(&self: source'[a], from: Size, to: Size) -> &source'[a]:
    let &start = from
    if start < 0 then start = 0
    if start > self.length then start = self.length

    let &end = to
    if end < start then end = start
    if end > self.length then end = self.length

    return ArrayView {items: advance(self.items, start), length: end - start}

{-
   The derivations - how an owner and a fixed array become the window a `[a]` position holds.

   Ordinary instances of the classes `Core.yana` declares, and the compiler's one adaptation site
   calls whichever of them the position's access names. Each body builds an `ArrayView` and hands it
   back at a result position that is a window, which is the one place a view record may go; the
   result is rooted in `self` by the signature, exactly as a returned borrow is anywhere else.

   A fixed array's base is its own address, so the pointer is the array's read as an element
   pointer, and its count is the type's - `n :: Size` is the constant `length` on a fixed array
   answers, with no window built at all.
-}
{-
   The runs, which for a contiguous container is one run - `lib/Core/Container.yana`'s two
   `Chunked` instances, verbatim.

   One `yield` of the whole window, so `for x in items(xs)` over an array is the chunk loop with a
   single iteration and the element loop inside it, and every pass between here and the backend sees
   the shape it would have seen if the loop had been written by hand.

   The array's yields `self` where `lib/Core/Container.yana` yields `elements(self)`, and that is
   this stage's model showing rather than a shortcut: `self: [a]` **is** the window here - `Array(a)`
   under a view contract - so there is nothing left to derive, and calling the owner's `elements` on
   one would hand a view to a position that holds the container. A fixed array is the case that does
   have something to derive, and it calls the derivation.
-}
instance Chunked(Array(a), a):
    iter fn chunks(self: source'[a]) -> source'[a]:
        yield self

instance (n: Int) Chunked([a *n], a):
    iter fn chunks(self: source'[a *n]) -> source'[a]:
        yield elements(self)

instance Contiguous(Array(a), a):
    @inline(always) fn elements(self: source'Array(a)) -> source'[a] =
        ArrayView {items: self.run.items, length: truncate(self.length) :: Size}

instance Writable(Array(a), a):
    @inline(always) fn elementsMut(&self: source'Array(a)) -> &source'[a] =
        ArrayView {items: self.run.items, length: truncate(self.length) :: Size}

instance (n: Int) Contiguous([a *n], a):
    @inline(always) fn elements(self: source'[a *n]) -> source'[a] =
        ArrayView {items: bitcast(addressOf(self)) :: %a, length: n :: Size}

instance (n: Int) Writable([a *n], a):
    @inline(always) fn elementsMut(&self: source'[a *n]) -> &source'[a] =
        ArrayView {items: bitcast(addressOf(self)) :: %a, length: n :: Size}

{-
   The owner's operations - `lib/Core/Array.native.yana`'s, over the same two fields.

   Every one is a body over `run` and `length`, and none of it is the compiler's: the growth is
   `Run`'s `resize`, the store is `store` through the run's pointer, and a push that could not get
   the room stops the program the way a bounds failure does. `push` takes its item by `->` because
   the array takes it - the store through the raw pointer is the hand-over, and the frame that owned
   the item stops owing it a teardown on the path that stored.
-}
pub fn emptyArray() -> Array(a) = Array {run: emptyRun(), length: 0}

pub fn ofCapacity(capacity: Size) -> Array(a) = Array {run: newRun(capacity), length: 0}

{-
   **`capacity(self: Array(a))` is deliberately not here**, and `lib/Core/Array.native.yana` has one.

   `doc/spec/overloading.md` R1 keys an overload set by `(name, arity)` and lets it hold at most one
   plain function, and `Native.capacity` is already a one-argument `capacity`. Declaring the array's
   beside it makes the name ambiguous in every module that imports both - which is what the real
   library does today and what the shipping stage does not report. Recorded rather than worked
   around: the name is the library's to sort out, and `self.run.capacity` is what this file reads in
   the meantime.
-}

pub fn reserve(&self: Array(a), wanted: Size):
    if wanted <= capacity(self.run) then return
    let room = capacity(self.run)
    let &wide = room + room
    if wide < wanted then wide = wanted
    if wide < 4 then wide = 4
    if !resize(self.run, wide) then checkFailed()

{-
   The array a literal is built into - `[1, 2, 3]`, and the second half of what `values` above is the
   first half of.

   `count` slots, already **live**: the length says they hold values before any of them does, which
   is the one thing a caller may not do and the one thing the compiler must. It writes every slot
   immediately, in index order, with nothing of the program's own running in between - so the only
   window in which the length is a lie is one no code is in.

   That is why this is here rather than expressed as `ofCapacity` and a run of `push`es. Those two
   are the same three field writes done `count` times over, each re-reading a length the caller
   already knows; and the elements would be handed over one at a time to a function, where what a
   literal has is `count` values in hand and one place to put them.

   A refusal is `checkFailed` and not a shorter array. `ofCapacity` answers the empty run when the
   allocator says no - the caller that needed the room tests for it, which is what `push` does - and
   a literal has no such caller: the slots are about to be written whatever this said, so the length
   and the capacity have to agree here or not at all.
-}
@builtin(arrayLiteral)
pub fn literalArray(count: Size) -> Array(a):
    let run = newRun(count) :: Run(a)
    if capacity(run) < count then checkFailed()
    return Array {run: run, length: truncate(count) :: Count}

pub fn push(&self: Array(a), ->item: a):
    let count = truncate(self.length) :: Size
    reserve(self, count + 1)
    store(advance(self.run.items, count), item)
    self.length = truncate(count + 1) :: Count

{-
   Taking one element out and closing the gap - `lib/Core/Array.native.yana`'s `remove`.

   The element comes back rather than being dropped, which is what makes this usable for a type that
   owns something: the caller decides what happens to it, and `Nothing` is the answer for an index
   that names no element. One unsigned comparison does both bounds, which is the same trick every
   subscript here uses - a negative index reinterpreted unsigned is above every length there is.

   **The tail is shifted element by element** where `lib` calls `moveMemory`. That function is a
   vector ladder and is not in this library, and what the loop says instead is exactly what the block
   move would have done: element `i + 1` is taken out and put at `i`, from the hole upwards, so every
   slot holds a live value before and after except the one the caller now owns.

   The moves are through the run's own pointer, which is unchecked by construction and correct for
   the reason the bounds test establishes: every index below `length` names a live element.
-}
pub fn remove(&self: Array(a), index: Int) -> Maybe(a):
    let count = truncate(self.length) :: Size
    let at = index :: Size
    if (bitcast(at) :: USize) >= (bitcast(count) :: USize) then return Nothing

    let ->doomed = *advance(self.run.items, at)

    let &i = at
    while i + 1 < count:
        store(advance(self.run.items, i), *advance(self.run.items, i + 1))
        i = i + 1

    self.length = truncate(count - 1) :: Count
    return Just(doomed)

{-
   The teardown - one traversal over the live elements, and the release of the run. Which halves of
   the ownership split this supplies is computed from the element type, as `lib/` says: an
   `Array(Int)` has nothing to run per element and this folds to the release.
-}
instance Reclaim(Array(a)):
    fn reclaim(->value: Array(a)):
        let count = truncate(value.length) :: Size
        let &i = 0 :: Size
        while i < count:
            let ->doomed = *advance(value.run.items, i)
            i = i + 1
        releaseRun(value.run)
